Project description
A delivery company has a service network of 30 customer sites. On any single day, not every site has some demand, i.e., the company only needs to serve a subset of the 30 sites. As a result, the vehicle routing would be different from day to day. Assume that the customers follow periodic demand that repeats week by week.

The company takes a zoning approach in which the sites are grouped into 3 fixed zones. Each zone is served by one delivery person who can decide the daily vehicle routing within the zone. It is allowed to have overlap between the zones, e.g., a customer can belong to both zone 1 and zone 2, and hence be served by different delivery staff on different days. The zones cannot change once they are fixed.

We need to help the company design the zones, aiming to minimizing the total delivery cost of the week.

We have collected some basic information.

1. Each site j, j=1,…, 30, as well as the hub referred as site 0, has known (x,y) coordinates. The cost between two sites can be measured by their Manhattan distance.
2. Each zone cannot have more than 12 sites.
3. Vehicle capacity is not an issue.

======
approach result with the IEDA 3410 project code
Zone 1: Customers mostly clustered in one geographic area.
Zone 2: Another distinct cluster of customers.
Zone 3: Overlapping customers from Zones 1 and 2 plus some unique ones, enabling flexible daily assignments.

The total cost of 3044 represents the sum of daily routing costs (travel distances) across all zones and days Monday to Friday